WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.432
Two sets of points lie in a plane.

2
00:00:06.882 --> 00:00:12.709
Pair them up, one to one, so that the total squared
distance between partners is as small as possible.

3
00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:18.160
Now let one set turn about its centre.
As it turns, the best pairing changes.

4
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.948
In 2008, the mathematician
Günter Rote asked:

5
00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:28.286
during one full turn, how many
different best pairings can there be?

6
00:00:28.736 --> 00:00:32.021
The number of pairings grows
like n factorial. For ten points,

7
00:00:32.041 --> 00:00:34.483
that is more than three and a half million.

8
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:40.603
The key is a change of picture. Each
pairing becomes a single point in the plane.

9
00:00:41.653 --> 00:00:48.155
Turning the set sweeps a direction around this cloud, and the
best pairing is always the point farthest in that direction.

10
00:00:48.605 --> 00:00:53.105
So only corners of the outline can ever
win. Here, twelve of the twenty-four.

11
00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:59.457
The answer: the outline never has
more than n times n minus one corners,

12
00:00:59.477 --> 00:01:02.776
wherever the points are
placed. For ten points, ninety.

13
00:01:04.826 --> 00:01:06.132
Nothing smaller would do.

14
00:01:06.152 --> 00:01:11.659
Put the fixed points on a line, and the best pairing
simply sorts the moving points by their shadows.

15
00:01:12.009 --> 00:01:16.618
Each pair of shadows changes order twice
per turn. Six pairs, twelve changes:

16
00:01:16.638 --> 00:01:19.914
the simplest arrangement
is already the extreme one.

17
00:01:21.564 --> 00:01:24.024
The count also has a reason. In one turn,

18
00:01:24.044 --> 00:01:28.791
every pair of moving points turns once
around. Six pairs: twelve half-turns.

19
00:01:29.241 --> 00:01:31.566
Follow the points by their current partners.

20
00:01:31.586 --> 00:01:36.736
That arrangement always stays where its pairing
is optimal, a region with no hole to wind around.

21
00:01:36.756 --> 00:01:42.182
So all the turning must be given back by switches,
and each switch gives back at least half a turn.

22
00:01:42.632 --> 00:01:46.494
Twelve half-turns, twelve
switches. The account balances.

23
00:01:48.344 --> 00:01:51.252
The reason is planar, and
so is the result. In space,

24
00:01:51.272 --> 00:01:58.992
directions can travel without winding, and four points turning about
an axis give thirteen best pairings, one more than the plane allows.

25
00:02:00.842 --> 00:02:02.422
One practical consequence:

26
00:02:02.442 --> 00:02:12.069
the best rigid alignment of two planar point sets with unknown correspondence
can be computed exactly, by checking at most n times n minus one candidates.
